{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 415 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 415 "Error, (in Member) th
e second argument must be a set or a list" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com
/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Error,%20(in%20Member)%20the%20sec
ond%20argument%20must%20be%20a%20set%20or%20a%20list" "" }{TEXT 415 0 
"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 59 "Alternative we could have w
ritten the procedure as follows:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT
 1 0 40 "Member:=proc(x::anything,Y::\{set,list\})\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
11 "  local i;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 96 "  description \"this procedure ret
urns true if x is anything, Y is a set or list and x is in Y\";\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 29 "  for i from 1 to nops(Y) do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 42 "   \+
 if x = Y[i] then return true; end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end do;
\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "  false;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end proc:" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "Member(2,\{1,2,3\});" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "I%trueG%*protectedG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "
> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "Member(2,[1,2,3]);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "I%trueG%*protectedG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT
 1 0 18 "Member(2,2*x^3+3);" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 415 0 "" }
{HYPERLNK 415 "Error, invalid input: Member expects its 2nd argument, \+
Y, to be of type \{list, set\}, but received 2*x^3+3" 4 "http://www.ma
plesoft.com/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Error,%20invalid%20inpu
t%3A%20Member%20expects%20its%202nd%20argument,%20Y,%20to%20be%20of%20
type%20%7Blist,%20set%7D,%20but%20received%202*x%5E3+3" "" }{TEXT 415 
0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 106 "Note Well: You don't hav
e to construct your own member procedure. You may use the Maple builti
n procedure " }{TEXT 250 7 "member " }{TEXT 414 38 "or for more genera
l uses the procedure" }{TEXT 251 5 " has." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 ""
 {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 41 "Recursive
 procedures: (Fibonacci numbers)" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 2 "A " }{TEXT 252 9 "recursive" }
{TEXT 414 48 " procedure is a procedure which \"calls itself\". " }
{TEXT 253 85 "This means that the name of the procedure is used in the
 definition of the procedure." }{TEXT 414 64 "  In this section we giv
e examples of such. First a definition. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 
0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 4 "The " }{TEXT 254 17 "Fibonacci num
bers" }{TEXT 414 184 ":  The Fibonacci numbers form a sequence 0, 1, 1
, 2, 3, 5,  8, 13, ...  It starts with 0, 1 and thereafter the n-th te
rm is the sum of the (n-2)-nd and (n-1)-st terms in the sequence. " }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 136 "We wil
l show several ways to produce Fibonacci numbers. Each procedure will \+
give the n-th Fibonacci number. We call the first procedure " }{TEXT 
255 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 414 12 ". Note that " }{TEXT 256 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 
414 36 " is used twice in the definition of " }{TEXT 257 4 "fib1" }
{TEXT 414 2 ". " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 ">
 " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "restart;\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "fib1:=proc(n)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 27 " if n = 0 or n = 1
 then n;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 33 "   else fib1(n-2) + fib1(n-1);  \n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 " end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "end proc:\n" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "fib1(2);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "\"\"\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "fib1(
3);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\"#" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 21 "seq(fib1(i),i=0..20);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 
20 "67\"\"!\"\"\"F$\"\"#\"\"$\"\"&\"\")\"#8\"#@\"#M\"#b\"#*)\"$W\"\"$L
#\"$x$\"$5'\"$()*\"%(f\"\"%%e#\"%\"=%\"%ln" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 414 24 "Note from n = 2 onward, " }{TEXT 258 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 
414 16 "(n) is equal to " }{TEXT 259 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 414 8 "(n-2) + " 
}{TEXT 260 3 "fib" }{TEXT 414 0 "" }{TEXT 261 1 "1" }{TEXT 414 28 "(n-
1). Note that to compute " }{TEXT 262 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 414 6 "(20), " }
{TEXT 263 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 414 20 " must first compute " }{TEXT 264 4 "
fib1" }{TEXT 414 9 "(19) and " }{TEXT 265 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 414 52 "(18)
,  but to compute each of these it must compute " }{TEXT 266 4 "fib1" 
}{TEXT 414 9 "(18) and " }{TEXT 267 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 414 9 "(17) and " 
}{TEXT 268 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 414 9 "(17) and " }{TEXT 269 4 "fib1" }
{TEXT 414 54 "(16), and so on. This takes a lot of time and storage." 
}}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 414 14 "By putting in " }{TEXT 270 15 "option remember" }{TEXT 
414 30 ", as we now do, the procedure " }{TEXT 271 9 "remembers" }
{TEXT 414 66 " the values already computed -- it puts them in a table,
 called a " }{TEXT 272 14 "remember table" }{TEXT 414 140 "-- and does
 not have to recompute them. The only problem with this is it sometime
s uses a lot of memory to store the computed values in the " }{TEXT 
273 15 "remember table." }{TEXT 414 20 " We illustrate with " }{TEXT 
274 4 "fib2" }{TEXT 414 2 ":\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 14 "fib2:=proc(n)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "  option remember:\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 29 "  if n = 0 or n = 1 then n; \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 33 "   \+
 else fib2(n-2) + fib2(n-1); \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end if;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "end proc:\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 275 28 "
Comparison of running times." }{TEXT 414 11 " Note that " }{TEXT 276 
6 "time()" }{TEXT 414 188 "; gives the  total CPU time used since the \+
start of the Maple session your computer in seconds. So the following \+
method gives the time used for the given computation. Note how much fa
ster " }{TEXT 277 4 "fib2" }{TEXT 414 4 " is." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT
 414 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "starttime:=time
():\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "  fib1(25);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 31 "elapsedtime:
=time()-starttime;\n" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"&D](" }}{PARA 
11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I,elapsedtimeG6\"$\"$D\"!\"$" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "starttime:=time():\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
0 12 "  fib2(25);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 31 "elapsedtime:=time()-starttime;
\n" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"&D](" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 ">I,elapsedtimeG6\"$\"\"!F&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 414 65 " Here's how we find the values stored in the remember ta
ble. For " }{TEXT 278 4 "fib1" }{TEXT 414 31 " there is no remember ta
ble:   " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 17 "op(4,eval(fib1))
;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 4 "But " }{TEXT 279 4 "fib2" }
{TEXT 414 46 " has a large remember table after calculating " }{TEXT 
280 8 "fib2(25)" }{TEXT 414 1 ":" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT
 1 0 19 "op(4,eval(fib2));  " }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "KI&TABLE
G%*protectedG6#7</\"\"!F(/\"\"\"F*/\"\"#F*/\"\"$F,/\"\"&F0/\"\"%F./\"
\"(\"#8/\"\"'\"\")/\"#5\"#b/\"#6\"#*)/F8\"#@/\"\"*\"#M/\"#:\"$5'/\"#9
\"$x$/F5\"$L#/\"#7\"$W\"/F@\"&Y4\"/\"#?\"%ln/\"#B\"&d'G/\"#A\"&6x\"/\"
#;\"$()*/\"#<\"%(f\"/\"#=\"%%e#/\"#>\"%\"=%/\"#C\"&oj%/\"#D\"&D](Q(ppr
int06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 21 "Let's try to compute 
" }{TEXT 281 10 "fib2(4000)" }{TEXT 414 58 ". I suppress the output si
nce it is a rather large number." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT
 1 0 19 "starttime:=time():\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "fib2(4000):\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 30 "elapsedtime:=time()-starttime;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 ">I,elapsedtimeG6\"$\"\"!F&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 414 17 "Now the value of " }{TEXT 282 10 "fib2(4000)" }{TEXT 
414 95 " is in the remember table so it doesn't have to be recomputed.
 Thus it is returned immediately:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "starttime:=time():\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "fib2(4000):\n
" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 30 "elapsedtime:=time()-starttime;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 ""
 {XPPMATH 20 ">I,elapsedtimeG6\"$\"\"!F&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 414 10 "Note that " }{TEXT 283 4 "fib2" }{TEXT 414 22 "(4000) is
 quicker the " }{TEXT 284 6 "second" }{TEXT 414 50 " time you call it \+
since it has already calculated " }{TEXT 285 4 "fib2" }{TEXT 414 35 "(
4000) and just looks it up in its " }{TEXT 286 16 "remember table. " }
{TEXT 414 88 "Such tables can clog up one's memory so to clear it out \+
when no longer needed using the " }{TEXT 287 6 "forget" }{TEXT 414 20 
" command as follows:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "fo
rget(fib2);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 57 "See that the reme
mber table for fib2 is really empty now:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "op(4,eval(fib2));\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
414 276 "Recursive procedures can be written as non-recursive procedur
es--although usually with more effort. For example,  here is a way to \+
generate the Fibonacci numbers by a procedure which doesn't call itsel
f. It is a little harder to understand, but requires much less memory \+
than " }{TEXT 288 6 "fib2. " }{TEXT 414 9 "I call it" }{TEXT 289 7 " f
ib3. " }{TEXT 290 50 "Read it carefully and try to see what it is doin
g." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "fib3:=proc(n)\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 30 "  local minus1,minus2,temp,i;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "  \+
if n = 0 or n = 1 then \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "    return n; \n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "  minus2:=0;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "  minus1:=1:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 23 "  for i from 2 to n
 do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 27 "    temp:=minus2 + minus1;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
20 "    minus2:=minus1;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "    minus1:=temp;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "  temp;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
0 9 "end proc:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 21 "seq(fib3(
i),i=0..20);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "67\"\"!\"\"\"F$\"\"#\"\"
$\"\"&\"\")\"#8\"#@\"#M\"#b\"#*)\"$W\"\"$L#\"$x$\"$5'\"$()*\"%(f\"\"%%
e#\"%\"=%\"%ln" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 37 "Compare the ti
me it takes to compute " }{TEXT 291 10 "fib3(1000)" }{TEXT 414 35 " wi
th the time it takes to compute " }{TEXT 292 8 "fib1(20)" }{TEXT 414 
5 " and " }{TEXT 293 10 "fib2(1000)" }{TEXT 414 71 ": Notice which is \+
the fastest.  Note that we don't dare try to compute " }{TEXT 294 10 "
fib1(1000)" }{TEXT 414 23 " due to how slow it is." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "starttime:=time():\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "fib
3(1000);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 32 "elapsedtime:=time() - starttime;" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\\xv)G#\\o;&zPhZ/P+;&G*\\'p*)HQz=Ll!4W
'*Hkh6ZAL()RKi*o4_xMEK!3$fAfHp^Dz+%)zQS!*=0\\zTbl`*3H<[-/yrt^gYc-eiS]n
F&)oNkXPpodlYV" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I,elapsedtimeG6\"$\"#
;!\"$" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "starttime:=time():
\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "fib1(0);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 32 "elapsedtime:=time()
 - starttime;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\"!" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 
"" {XPPMATH 20 ">I,elapsedtimeG6\"$\"\"!F&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "starttime:=time():\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "fib2(1000);
\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 32 "elapsedtime:=time() - starttime;" }}{PARA 11 "" 
1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\\xv)G#\\o;&zPhZ/P+;&G*\\'p*)HQz=Ll!4W'*Hkh6ZAL()R
Ki*o4_xMEK!3$fAfHp^Dz+%)zQS!*=0\\zTbl`*3H<[-/yrt^gYc-eiS]nF&)oNkXPpodl
YV" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I,elapsedtimeG6\"$\"\"!F&" }}}
