}}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}}{SECT 0 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 
41 "Two more examples of recursive procedures" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "
" {TEXT 414 91 "Study carefully the procedures ADD and MUL defined in \+
this section. The special identifier " }{TEXT 297 8 "procname" }{TEXT 
414 119 " to make the recursive calls, which allows the name of the pr
ocedure to be changed without changing the procedure body." }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "restart:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 38 "ADD:=proc(f::procedure, n::nonnegint)\n" }{MPLTEXT 
1 0 19 "  option remember;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 68 "  description `this co
mputes the sum f(0) + f(1) + f(2) +...+f(n)`;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 30 "  i
f n = 0 then return f(0); \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 39 "    else return procna
me(f,n-1)+f(n); \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "en
d proc:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "ADD(cos,4);" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ",,\"\"\"F#-I$cosG6$%*protectedGI(_syslib
G6\"6#F#F#-F%6#\"\"#F#-F%6#\"\"$F#-F%6#\"\"%F#" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> "
 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "ADD(cos,-4);" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 415 0 "
" }{HYPERLNK 415 "Error, invalid input: ADD expects its 2nd argument, \+
n, to be of type nonnegint, but received -4" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.c
om/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Error,%20invalid%20input%3A%20AD
D%20expects%20its%202nd%20argument,%20n,%20to%20be%20of%20type%20nonne
gint,%20but%20received%20-4" "" }{TEXT 415 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> "
 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 38 "MUL:=proc(f::procedure, n::nonnegint)\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "  option remember;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 67 "  description
 `this computes the product f(0)*f(1)*f(2)*...*f(n)`;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
0 30 "  if n = 0 then return f(0); \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 39 "    else retu
rn procname(f,n-1)*f(n); \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "  end if; \n" }{MPLTEXT
 1 0 9 "end proc:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "MUL(ex
p,4);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "**-I$expG6$%*protectedGI(_sysli
bG6\"6#\"\"\"F*-F$6#\"\"#F*-F$6#\"\"$F*-F$6#\"\"%F*" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0
 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "MUL(g,5);" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 415 0 
"" }{HYPERLNK 415 "Error, invalid input: MUL expects its 1st argument,
 f, to be of type procedure, but received g" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.c
om/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Error,%20invalid%20input%3A%20MU
L%20expects%20its%201st%20argument,%20f,%20to%20be%20of%20type%20proce
dure,%20but%20received%20g" "" }{TEXT 415 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "ADD(g,5);" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 415 0 "" }
{HYPERLNK 415 "Error, invalid input: ADD expects its 1st argument, f, \+
to be of type procedure, but received g" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/s
upport/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Error,%20invalid%20input%3A%20ADD%20
expects%20its%201st%20argument,%20f,%20to%20be%20of%20type%20procedure
,%20but%20received%20g" "" }{TEXT 415 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 ""
 {TEXT 416 77 "We can make Maple think g is a procedure if we execute \+
the following command:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "g(
-1):=2;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">-I\"gG6\"6#!\"\"\"\"#" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 112 "Note that this not only makes g \+
into a procedure, but sets up a remember table for it containing the e
ntry -1=2." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "op(4,eval(g))
;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "KI&TABLEG%*protectedG6#7#/!\"\"\"\"
#Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "MUL(g,5);" 
}}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "*.-I\"gG6\"6#\"\"!\"\"\"-F$6#F(F(-F$6#
\"\"#F(-F$6#\"\"$F(-F$6#\"\"%F(-F$6#\"\"&F(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 
"" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "ADD(g,5);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ",.-I\"gG
6\"6#\"\"!\"\"\"-F$6#F(F(-F$6#\"\"#F(-F$6#\"\"$F(-F$6#\"\"%F(-F$6#\"\"
&F(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "
" 0 "" {TEXT 414 114 "Both procedures can also be written with a do lo
op as we have seen before. Actually Maple has built-in procedures " }
{TEXT 298 3 "sum" }{TEXT 414 2 ", " }{TEXT 299 3 "add" }{TEXT 414 2 ",
 " }{TEXT 300 7 "product" }{TEXT 414 5 " and " }{TEXT 301 3 "mul" }
{TEXT 414 178 " which do more or less what these procedures do (and ar
e more efficiently) as we will discuss in the next lecture. These two \+
procedures were just for the purpose of illustration." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 
"" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 302 4 "NOTE" }{TEXT 414 34 "
: Executing a command of the form " }{TEXT 303 11 "name(x):=y " }{TEXT
 414 5 "make " }{TEXT 304 4 "name" }{TEXT 414 58 " into a procedure wh
ereas executing a command of the form " }{TEXT 305 10 "name[x]:=y" }
{TEXT 414 7 " makes " }{TEXT 306 4 "name" }{TEXT 414 14 " into a table
." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
413 25 "Viewing maple source code" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT
 1 0 8 "restart:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 114 "For program
mers, a tremendous benefit of Maple is its \"open-architecture\". You \+
can access the source code to over " }{TEXT 206 3 "95%" }{TEXT 414 
217 " of the math routines available.  This allows you to see what's g
oing on and in some cases you may want to change procedures to make th
em more suitable for your needs.   One way to view the code is to use \+
the command " }{TEXT 307 8 "showstat" }{TEXT 414 95 " as follows. This
 is also useful for debugging. Note that it numbers the lines of the p
rogram. " }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "showstat(rand);" }}
{PARA 6 "" 1 "" {TEXT 417 1 "\n" }{TEXT 417 49 "rand := proc(r::\{posi
nt, numeric .. numeric\}, $)\n" }{TEXT 417 25 "   1   if nargs = 0 the
n\n" }{TEXT 417 59 "   2       RandomTools:-MersenneTwister:-GenerateI
nteger()\n" }{TEXT 417 12 "       else\n" }{TEXT 417 15 "   3       tr
y\n" }{TEXT 417 58 "   4           if type(r,('numeric') .. ('numeric'
)) then\n" }{TEXT 417 43 "   5               if rhs(r) < lhs(r) then\n
" }{TEXT 417 74 "   6                   error \"invalid range, end poi
nts are out of order\"\n" }{TEXT 417 68 "                   elif not t
ype(r,('integer') .. ('integer')) then\n" }{TEXT 417 105 "   7        \+
           return () -> RandomTools:-Generate(float(('range') = r,('me
thod') = ('uniform')))\n" }{TEXT 417 26 "                   end if\n" 
}{TEXT 417 23 "               end if;\n" }{TEXT 417 80 "   8          \+
 return RandomTools:-MersenneTwister:-NewGenerator(('range') = r)\n" }
{TEXT 417 19 "           catch :\n" }{TEXT 417 21 "   9           erro
r\n" }{TEXT 417 19 "           end try\n" }{TEXT 417 14 "       end if
\n" }{TEXT 417 9 "end proc\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 1 "
\n" }{TEXT 414 26 "Another way is to use the " }{TEXT 308 10 "interfac
e " }{TEXT 414 37 "command as follows. First we execute:" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "interface(verboseproc=2);" }}{PARA 
11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\"\"" }}}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 96 "Then we can view the code for var
ious  procedures. For example, let's look at isprime. Applying " }
{TEXT 309 5 "eval " }{TEXT 414 4 " or " }{TEXT 310 6 "print " }{TEXT 
414 25 "returns the source code. " }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
15 "print(isprime);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "`6\"I,_m101064908
GF#6(I,ModuleApplyG6$I&:-234GQPC:\\Program~Files~(x86)\\Maple~2018\\li
b\\maple.mlaF#/I'_localG%*protectedGI,_n102351020GF#I*cyclotestG6$I&:-
235GF)/F+I,_n102351048GF#I(sumxtorG6$I&:-236GF)/F+I,_n101228980GF#I+Tr
aceModQFG6$I&:-237GF)/F+I,_n102351076GF#I\"iG6$I&:-238GF)/F+I,_n101064
928GF#I/special_primesG6$I&:-239GF)/F+I,_n104055076GF#" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 95 "You may find it difficult to copy and pa
ste from the output above.  If so use  use the command " }{TEXT 311 
22 "lprint(eval(isprime));" }{TEXT 414 88 " as follows. Then you shoul
d be able to select, copy and paste into Maple input regions." }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "lprint(eval(isprime));" }}
{PARA 6 "" 1 "" {TEXT 417 11 "_m101064908" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 414 156 "Now selecting the first large number in the program, we
 paste it in and factor it using ifactor. We see that it is the produc
t of all primes from 2 to 100. " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 47 "ifactor(2305567963945518424753102147331756070);" }}{PARA 11 ""
 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "*T-I!G6\"6#\"\"#\"\"\"-F$6#\"\"$F(-F$6#\"\"&F(-F$6#
\"\"(F(-F$6#\"#6F(-F$6#\"#8F(-F$6#\"#<F(-F$6#\"#>F(-F$6#\"#BF(-F$6#\"#
HF(-F$6#\"#JF(-F$6#\"#PF(-F$6#\"#TF(-F$6#\"#VF(-F$6#\"#ZF(-F$6#\"#`F(-
F$6#\"#fF(-F$6#\"#hF(-F$6#\"#nF(-F$6#\"#rF(-F$6#\"#tF(-F$6#\"#zF(-F$6#
\"#$)F(-F$6#\"#*)F(-F$6#\"#(*F(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 
46 "Notice that this procedure used the procedure " }{TEXT 312 4 "igcd
" }{TEXT 414 44 " as well as other procedures. The procedure " }{TEXT 
313 4 "igcd" }{TEXT 414 120 " is \"builtin\" and not available--as you
 see if you try the following command. A few of the more basic command
s are like " }{TEXT 314 4 "igcd" }{TEXT 414 44 " and the code is not a
vailable to the user. " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "e
val(igcd);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "f*6\"F#6#/I(builtinGF#I%ig
cdG%*protectedGF#F#F#F#F#" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
10 "eval(seq);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "f*6\"F#6#/I(builtinGF#
I$seqG%*protectedGF#F#F#F#F#" }}}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 65 "When done looking at the source c
ode it is a good idea to  reset " }{TEXT 315 11 "verboseproc" }{TEXT 
414 29 " to the default output level." }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
0 25 "interface(verboseproc=1);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\"#
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 3 ""
 0 "" {TEXT 413 12 "Option trace" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 8 "restart;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 316 14 "option trace:
 " }{TEXT 414 163 "This option causes maple to print out more details \+
about the behavior of the procedure and may be useful during debugging
. It seems to have the same effect as the " }{TEXT 317 14 "debug, unde
bug" }{TEXT 414 5 " and " }{TEXT 318 5 "trace" }{TEXT 414 2 ", " }
{TEXT 319 7 "untrace" }{TEXT 414 55 " command mentioned previously. He
re's a simple example." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "t
est:=proc(n)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "  local i,S;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "  \+
option trace;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "  S:=0:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 23 "  for i
 from 1 to n do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 34 "    if i > 2 then S:=S+i; end if;
\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 5 "  S;\n" }{MPLTEXT 
1 0 9 "end proc:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "test(6);
" }}{PARA 9 "" 1 "" {TEXT 418 25 "\{--> enter test, args = 6" }}{PARA 
11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I\"SG6#/I'_localG%*protectedGI+_n72669020G6\"
\"\"!" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I\"SG6#/I'_localG%*protectedGI
+_n72669020G6\"\"\"$" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I\"SG6#/I'_loca
lG%*protectedGI+_n72669020G6\"\"\"(" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">
I\"SG6#/I'_localG%*protectedGI+_n72669020G6\"\"#7" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 ">I\"SG6#/I'_localG%*protectedGI+_n72669020G6\"\"#=" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"#=" }}{PARA 9 "" 1 "" {TEXT 418 38 "<-
- exit test (now at top level) = 18\}" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 
"\"#=" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 31 "Remove or comment out t
he line " }{TEXT 320 12 "option trace" }{TEXT 414 56 "; and Re-enter t
he procedure to get back in normal mode." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "test:=proc(n)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "  local i,S;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 17 "  #option trace;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "  S:=0:\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 23 "  for i from 1 to n do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 34 "    if i \+
> 2 then S:=S+i; end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 
1 0 5 "  S;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end proc:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 ""
 {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "test(4);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\"(" }}}}
{SECT 0 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 15 " args and nargs" }}{EXCHG {PARA 
0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "restart;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
414 55 "The variables used as input for a procedure are called " }
{TEXT 321 10 "parameters" }{TEXT 414 4 " or " }{TEXT 322 9 "arguments"
 }{TEXT 414 56 ". For example, in the procedure whose definition begin
s " }{TEXT 323 19 "f:=proc(A,s,t,n),  " }{TEXT 414 28 "A, s,t and n ar
e called the " }{TEXT 324 9 "arguments" }{TEXT 414 4 " or " }{TEXT 
325 10 "parameters" }{TEXT 414 45 " of the procedure. By using the spe
cial name " }{TEXT 326 5 "args " }{TEXT 414 4 "and " }{TEXT 327 5 "nar
gs" }{TEXT 414 65 " one need not declare any parameters when defining \+
a procedure.  " }{TEXT 328 8 "args[1] " }{TEXT 414 23 "is the first ar
gument, " }{TEXT 329 8 "args[2] " }{TEXT 414 56 "is the second argumen
t, etc. The number of arguments is " }{TEXT 330 6 "nargs." }{TEXT 414 
25 "  One advantage in using " }{TEXT 331 4 "args" }{TEXT 414 5 " and 
" }{TEXT 332 5 "nargs" }{TEXT 414 88 " is that we don't have to specif
y in advance the number of arguments. Here's an example:" }}{PARA 0 ""
 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 53 "In this p
rocedure we use the builtin Maple procedure " }{TEXT 333 3 "add" }
{TEXT 210 2 ".\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "Avg:=pr
oc()\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "  local i,A,N;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 87 "  descri
ption \"this computes the average of the arguments--all assumed to be \+
numbers\";\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 21 "  if nargs = 0 then \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
46 "    error \"there are no numbers to average\"; \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
10 "  end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 30 "  A:=add(args[i],i=1..nargs);\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "  return evalf(A/nargs);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end proc
:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 17 "Avg(1,2,1,2,1,2);" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "$\"+++++:!\"*" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 
"" {MPLTEXT 1 0 20 "Avg(10.8,2,3,3,4,5);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 
20 "$\"+LLLLY!\"*" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 6 "Avg();"
 }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 415 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 415 "Error, (in Avg) th
ere are no numbers to average" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/hel
p/errors/view.aspx?path=Error,%20(in%20Avg)%20there%20are%20no%20numbe
rs%20to%20average" "" }{TEXT 415 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
414 123 "Next we show how to create a procedure to find the maximum of
 a sequence of numbers. Actually Maple has a builtin function " }{TEXT
 334 3 "max" }{TEXT 414 94 " to do this, but it is useful to know how \+
it is constructed for other purposes. Our procedure " }{TEXT 335 3 "Ma
x" }{TEXT 414 27 " returns the largest entry " }{TEXT 336 3 "and" }
{TEXT 414 95 " the index of the first occurrence of this entry. It doe
s a little more than Maple's procedure " }{TEXT 337 4 "max " }{TEXT 
414 28 "does, but it is not as fast." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "Max:=proc()\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "  local M,w,i;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 156 "  description `this procedure returns the list [M,w]
 where M is the largest element in the input sequence and w is the ind
ex of the first occurrence of M`;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "  M:=args[1];\n
" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "  w:=1;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 27 "  for i from 2 to narg
s do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 42 "    if args[i] > M then M:=args[i]; w:=i;\n"
 }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "    end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end do;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "  [M,w];\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "end proc:\n" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 27 "Max(10, 110 , 445, 78, 34);" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "7$\"$X%\"\"$" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 10 "Iteratio
