> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 1 " " }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 9 "E
xercises" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 210 1 "\n" }{TEXT 210 11 "Pro
blem 1. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
210 214 "If n people (numbered 1 to n) stand in a circle and someone s
tarts going around the circle and eliminating every other person till \+
only one person is left, the number J(n) of the person left at the end
 is given by " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 414 48 "    J(n) = 1                           if n = 1\n" }
{TEXT 414 56 "    J(n) = 2*J(n/2) - 1          if n > 1 and n is even
\n" }{TEXT 414 52 "    J(n) = 2*J((n-1)/2) + 1   if  n > 1 and n is od
d" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 12 "(i
) Write a " }{TEXT 365 9 "recursive" }{TEXT 366 1 " " }{TEXT 367 9 "pr
ocedure" }{TEXT 414 115 " to compute J. [As a check the first 16 value
s (starting with 1) of J(n) are 1,1,3,1,3,5,7,1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15,1]. \+
\n" }{TEXT 414 35 "(ii)Compute the value of J(10000). " }}{PARA 0 "" 0
 "" {TEXT 414 115 "(iii) Can you explain why this is so much faster th
an our recursive procedure to compute the n-th Fibonacci number?" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 1 "[
" }{TEXT 368 6 "Remark" }{TEXT 414 45 ". The problem above is a specia
l case of the " }{TEXT 369 16 "Josephus Problem" }{TEXT 414 171 ". Ins
tead of eliminating every other person, one could eliminate every 3rd \+
person, every 4th person, etc.  For more on the history and generaliza
tions of this problem see " }{TEXT 370 8 "Concrete" }{TEXT 414 1 " " }
{TEXT 371 11 "Mathematics" }{TEXT 414 4 " -- " }{TEXT 372 33 "A Founda
tion for Computer Science" }{TEXT 414 149 ", by Graham, Knuth and Pata
shnik, Chapter 1. You will also find a number of webpages and mathemat
ics papers devoted to this problem if you search on " }{TEXT 373 16 "J
osephus Problem" }{TEXT 414 28 " via a search engine or via " }{TEXT 
374 10 "MathSciNet" }{TEXT 414 13 " -- click on " }{TEXT 375 30 "Inter
esting Mathematical Links" }{TEXT 414 92 " at the bottom of the USF Ma
thematics Department's home page -- from a USF computer--to use " }
{TEXT 376 10 "MathSciNet" }{TEXT 414 2 ".]" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 210 11 "Problem 2. " }}{PARA 0 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 37 "Write a procedu
re using the variable " }{TEXT 377 4 "args" }{TEXT 414 57 " that will \+
take an unspecified finite number of numbers, " }{TEXT 378 35 "delete \+
the smallest and the largest" }{TEXT 414 2 ", " }{TEXT 379 62 "and ret
urn the average of the rest as a floating point number." }{TEXT 414 
131 " If there are fewer than 3 arguments have an error message say: \+
\"There are not enough arguments. There should be at least three.\" \n
" }{TEXT 414 1 "\n" }{TEXT 414 131 "You should have no input parameter
s in the definition of the procedure. You may write it directly or you
 may use the Maple command " }{TEXT 380 5 "sort " }{TEXT 414 30 "as a \+
part of your program. Do " }{TEXT 381 6 "?sort " }{TEXT 414 11 "to see
 how " }{TEXT 382 4 "sort" }{TEXT 414 77 " works. Test your procedure \+
with each of the following argument sequences:  \n" }{TEXT 414 1 "\n" 
}{TEXT 414 24 "   50,40,40, 40, 40, 10\n" }{TEXT 414 1 "\n" }{TEXT 
414 7 "   1,2\n" }{TEXT 414 1 "\n" }{TEXT 414 28 "   seq(100 - i, i = \+
1..100)\n" }{TEXT 414 1 "\n" }{TEXT 414 31 "   seq(modp(n,111), n=1..1
000)." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 210 
