{TEXT 410 0 "" }{TEXT 210 11 "Problem 5. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 256 "Show that if you increase th
e upper bound for n in Problem 4  from 20 to a higher value you will n
ot obtain any additional happy numbers less than 320. This also implie
s that the previously computed 50 happy numbers are the first 50 happy
 numbers, indeed. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 ""
 {TEXT 414 1 " " }{TEXT 411 5 "Hints" }{TEXT 414 31 ": Show that if in
 the sequence " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 1 "\n" }{TEXT 414 50 "    \+
         n, f(n), (f@@2)(n), . . ., (f@@20)(n)" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 414 0 "" }}}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 249 "a 4 is reached before
 a 1 is reached then a 1 will never be reached, so the number n will n
ot be happy. What happens to the sequence once 4 is reached? Look at s
ome examples. Then show that in 20 iterations a 1 or a 4 is reached fo
r n less than 320." }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 414 0 "" }}{PARA 0 
